Suppose that a constant $0\le L<\infty$ satisfies $$\label{eq:abstract-lipschitz}
 \sup_{i\in I}|b_i(t,y)-b_i(t,y')|
 \le L\sup_{j\in I}|y_j-y'_j|$$ for every $t\ge0$ and every $y,y'\in\mathbb R^I$ whose coordinate differences have finite supremum.

For $h\in\mathbb R^F$, let $$\mu_h(x)=\frac{\exp(\sum_{j\in F}h_jx_j)\mu(x)}
 {\sum_{z\in\{0,1\}^F}\exp(\sum_{j\in F}h_jz_j)\mu(z)},
 \qquad p_i(h)=\mathbb E_{\mu_h}X_i.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Let $H$ be a finite connected graph with at least two vertices, and let $\mathcal T(H)$ denote its set of spanning trees. For $h\in\mathbb R^{E(H)}$, give each $T\in\mathcal T(H)$ probability proportional to $\exp(\sum_{f\in T}h_f)$, and write $\xi_e=\mathbf 1_{\{e\in T\}}$ and $p_e(h)=\mathbb E_h\xi_e$. For every $e\in E(H)$, $$\label{eq:covariance-row}
\frac{\partial p_e}{\partial h_f}
 =\operatorname{Cov}_h(\xi_e,\xi_f),
\qquad
\sum_{f\in E(H)}
 \left|\frac{\partial p_e}{\partial h_f}\right|
 =2p_e(h)(1-p_e(h))\le\frac12.$$ In particular, for all $h,h'\in\mathbb R^{E(H)}$, $$\label{eq:tree-response}
 |p_e(h)-p_e(h')|
 \le\frac12\max_{f\in E(H)}|h_f-h'_f|.$$

The function $b_{e,S}$ takes values in $[0,1]$ and is continuous in $(t,(y_f)_{f\in S})$. For all $t\ge0$ and $y,y'\in\mathbb R^{E}$, $$\label{eq:finite-response}
 |b_{e,S}(t,y)-b_{e,S}(t,y')|
 \le\frac12\max_{f\in S}|y_f-y'_f|,$$ where the maximum over the empty set is defined to be zero.

Each $b_e$ is a jointly Borel function on $[0,\infty)\times\mathbb R^{E}$ with values in $[0,1]$, where $\mathbb R^{E}$ has its product Borel structure. The construction commutes with graph isomorphisms and does not select a root. For every $t\ge0$ and $y,y'\in\mathbb R^{E}$ such that $\sup_{f\in E}|y_f-y'_f|<\infty$, it satisfies $$\label{eq:response-bound}
 \sup_{e\in E}|b_e(t,y)-b_e(t,y')|
 \le\frac12\sup_{f\in E}|y_f-y'_f|.$$ Neither $y$ nor $y'$ is required to have bounded coordinates.

Let $b:[0,\infty)\times\mathbb R^I\longrightarrow[0,1]^I$ be jointly Borel. Suppose that a constant $0\le L<\infty$ satisfies $$\label{eq:abstract-lipschitz}
 \sup_{i\in I}|b_i(t,y)-b_i(t,y')|
 \le L\sup_{j\in I}|y_j-y'_j|$$ for every $t\ge0$ and every $y,y'\in\mathbb R^I$ whose coordinate differences have finite supremum. Then, for every $w\in\mathcal C_I$, there is a unique $Z(w)\in\mathcal C_I$ satisfying $$\label{eq:inverse-equation}
 Z_i(w)(t)=w_i(t)+\int_0^t b_i(s,Z(w)(s))\,ds,
 \qquad i\in I,\quad t\ge0.$$ The map $Z:\mathcal C_I\longrightarrow\mathcal C_I$ is Borel and causal: its restriction to $[0,T]$ depends only on the input restricted to $[0,T]$. It commutes with every coordinate relabeling under which the drift is equivariant.

Let $\nu$ be a probability law on $\{0,1\}^I$, where $I$ is countable, and for each $i$ choose finite sets $S_n(i)$ increasing to $I$. Interpret the finite-tilt functions <a href="#eq:finite-tilt" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:finite-tilt">[eq:finite-tilt]</a> and the limsup drift <a href="#eq:drift" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:drift">[eq:drift]</a> with this law and these sets. Suppose that the finite-tilt functions $b_{i,S_n(i)}(t,\cdot)$ are $L$-Lipschitz in the supremum norm on their finite fields, with one constant $0\le L<\infty$ for all $i,n$ and $t\ge0$. Then the drift has the Borel solution map of Lemma <a href="#lem:inverse" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:inverse">11</a>, and the Borel map $\Psi:\mathcal C_I\longrightarrow\{0,1\}^I$ defined by $$\label{eq:readout}
 \Psi_i(w)=
 \mathbf 1\left\{\limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{Z_i(w)(n)}{n}>\frac12\right\}$$ sends the law of independent standard Brownian paths to $\nu$. The map $\Psi$ inherits every coordinate relabeling equivariance of the drift.

Let $F$ be a nonempty finite set and let $\mu$ be a strongly Rayleigh probability law on $\{0,1\}^F$. For $h\in\mathbb R^F$, let $$\mu_h(x)=\frac{\exp(\sum_{j\in F}h_jx_j)\mu(x)}
 {\sum_{z\in\{0,1\}^F}\exp(\sum_{j\in F}h_jz_j)\mu(z)},
 \qquad p_i(h)=\mathbb E_{\mu_h}X_i.$$ For every $i,j\in F$, the derivative identity below holds, and for every $i\in F$ the row satisfies $$\label{eq:sr-covariance-row}
 \frac{\partial p_i}{\partial h_j}
 =\mathop{\mathrm{Cov}}_{\mu_h}(X_i,X_j),
 \qquad
 \sum_{j\in F}\left|\frac{\partial p_i}{\partial h_j}\right|
 \le 2p_i(h)(1-p_i(h))\le\frac12.$$ Consequently $$\label{eq:sr-response}
 |p_i(h)-p_i(h')|\le\frac12\max_{j\in F}|h_j-h'_j|
 \qquad(h,h'\in\mathbb R^F).$$
